"On the SPOT: Enhancing Speculative Decodingfor Diffusion Language Modelsvia Commit-Order Prediction (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22543)\nciteturn41259view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22543\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1284\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn41259view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22543v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRank Measures of Fine-Tuning UpdatesNeed a Noise Baseline (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22438v1)\nciteturn41259view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22438v1\",\"lineno\":105}); Total lines: 624\nL74: Real updates are full rank in the strict sense, so these readings rest on softer measures: the numerical rank, the number of directions that hold 90% of the energy (cite52†Biderman et al., 2024 ), or the entropy effective rank (cite54†Roy and Vetterli, 2007 ; cite51†Wu et al., 2025 ). cite52†Biderman et al. (2024) also compared against noise: the spectrum of a fully fine-tuned weight matrix looks like that of the pretrained one plus low-magnitude Gaussian noise, and they concluded that nothing about it is unusual.\nL75: We began this project with the same impression. Counting the singular values of a supervised fine-tuning (SFT) update above 1% of the largest gave 97% of full rank, and the difference between two random initialisations of the same model, which is pure noise, gave 99.6%. By that measure, fine-tuning looks almost like noise.\nL76: It is not. Stable rank (cite55†Rudelson and Vershynin, 2007 ), computed from the same singular values, puts the update 4.5 times below noise, and its top direction holds 6.6 times as much of the energy (4.2% against 0.6%). The two answers differ because the measures weigh different parts of the spectrum, and because measures of $W$ cannot see much of $\\Delta W$ at all.\nL77: Comparing against noise is an old idea in statistics (cite56†Horn, 1965 ; cite57†Gavish and Donoho, 2014 ), has been used to study trained weights (cite58†Martin and Mahoney, 2021 ; cite59†Thamm et al., 2022 ), and has been applied to fine-tuning updates before: cite60†Ryu et al. (2023) plot the spectrum of an update against a Marchenko–Pastur reference, and cite52†Biderman et al. (2024) against Gaussian noise.\nL78: What has not been done is to apply it measure by measure, which is where the disagreement is, with matched controls for what the update was trained on. That is what this paper does.\nL79: Our findings:\nL80: \nL81:   1. 1.\nL82: Counting measures compress the gap to noise. On our SFT, Wikipedia, code and direct preference optimisation (DPO) fine-tunes, the threshold count is only 2–5% below a random matrix of the same shape, and eRank and the 90% energy rank are 12–35% below it; stable rank is 78–94% below.\nL83: Along the whole family of Rényi ranks the gap grows with how much weight a measure gives to the top directions, then levels off, as it must for any spectrum with a heavier head than noise; the point is how large the disagreement is (§cite20†4.1 ).\nL84:   2. 2.\nL85: \nL86: Updates have a few strong directions and a long tail, and on average are more concentrated than the pretrained weights they are added to (§cite21†4.2 ).\nL87: \nL88:   3. 3.\nL89: The shape does not single out alignment training. With the amount of training held fixed, the data does shape an update (code is the least concentrated, the more so with more training), but instruction SFT does not stand out from Wikipedia or Python code. DPO gives the same shape, and the same directions, whether it learns real preferences or randomly swapped ones that teach nothing. Text with its word order scrambled gives the most concentrated update of all (§cite22†4.3 ).\nL90: \nL91:   4. 4.\nL92: It holds for released models, thirteen base/instruct, post-training-stage and base/specialist pairs up to 8B parameters. On three bf16 updates (Qwen2.5-1.5B and the OLMo 2 DPO and RL stages), much of what the threshold count counts may be rounding (§cite23†4.4 ).\nL93: \nL94:   5. 5.\nL95: It changes how earlier evidence reads. An unchanged effective rank of $W$ after post-training, read by cite51†Wu et al. (2025) as a sign that post-training is superficial, is what a random update of the same size also produces, even one that cuts the model’s GSM8K accuracy from 60% to 5%. The edit they build on the effective rank of the update has no measurable effect on GSM8K, nor, within about three points, on their own benchmark, GPQA-Diamond (§cite24†5 ).\nL96: The practical message is short: report rank measures of weight updates next to the same measure on noise, check that low-precision training or storage did not erase the update, and test claims about alignment against fine-tuning without alignment content.\nL97: ## 2 Measuring the rank of an update\nL98: \nL99: Let $\\Delta W\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times n}$ be the update to one weight matrix, $r=\\min(m,n)$, and $\\sigma_{1}\\geq\\dots\\geq\\sigma_{r}$ its singular values. Write $p_{i}=\\sigma_{i}^{2}/\\sum_{j}\\sigma_{j}^{2}$ for the share of the update’s energy (squared Frobenius norm) in direction $i$. We report every measure as a fraction of $r$, so that matrices of different shapes can be averaged.\nL100: ##### Five measures.\nL101: \nL102: In plain words, each asks “how many directions does this update really use?”, and they differ in how much the strongest directions count.\nL103: \nL104:   * •\nL105: \nL106: Threshold count $\\rho_{0.01}$: how many $\\sigma_{i}$ are at least $0.01\\,\\sigma_{1}$. We use it as the simplest example of counting; the numerical rank reported by cite53†Mukherjee et al. (2025) counts in the same way, with a tolerance they do not state (by default it is set by floating-point precision).\nL107: \nL108:   * •\nL109: Energy rank $k_{0.9}$: the smallest $k$ with $\\sum_{i\\leq k}p_{i}\\geq 0.9$, the measure of cite52†Biderman et al. (2024) .\nL110: \nL111:   * •\nL112: \nL113: Entropy effective rank $\\mathrm{eRank}$ (cite54†Roy and Vetterli, 2007 ): $\\exp\\bigl(-\\sum_{i}q_{i}\\log q_{i}\\bigr)$ with $q_{i}=\\sigma_{i}/\\sum_{j}\\sigma_{j}$, as used by cite51†Wu et al. (2025) .\nL114: \nL115:   * •\nL116: \nL117: Participation ratio: $1/\\sum_{i}p_{i}^{2}$.\nL118: \nL119:   * •\nL120: \nL121: Stable rank $\\mathrm{sr}$ (cite55†Rudelson and Vershynin, 2007 ): $\\|\\Delta W\\|_{F}^{2}/\\|\\Delta W\\|_{2}^{2}=1/p_{1}$.\nL122: All five ignore the overall scale of $\\Delta W$ (they are scale-invariant), which matters because the updates we compare differ in size by orders of magnitude.\nL123: ##### How the measures relate.\nL124: Three of the five are effective ranks of one kind: the exponential of a Rényi entropy of $p$ of order $\\alpha$, $R_{\\alpha}=\\bigl(\\sum_{i}p_{i}^{\\alpha}\\bigr)^{1/(1-\\alpha)}$. Order 0 counts the nonzero directions (the threshold count is a thresholded version of it), order 2 is the participation ratio, and order $\\infty$ is the stable rank. The higher the order, the more the top directions dominate.\nL125: This is why the measures can disagree about the same matrix: a spectrum with a few strong directions and a long tail of weak ones has many directions to count but most of its weight on top. The other two are close relatives rather than members: eRank uses the Shannon entropy of $q\\propto\\sigma$ instead of $p\\propto\\sigma^{2}$, and the energy rank counts directions up to a fixed share of $p$.\nL126: We list them between the threshold count and the participation ratio, which is where their values fall for our updates.\nL127: Note that $R_{\\alpha}$ can only fall as $\\alpha$ grows, for any matrix, so the order of the raw values tells us nothing. What does carry information is how far each measure puts an update below noise.\nL128: ##### The gap to noise.\nL129: For each measure we report $1-\\bar{x}_{\\text{update}}/\\bar{x}_{\\text{noise}}$, where $\\bar{x}$ is the mean of the measure over all matrices of a model, whatever their shape: 0 means the update looks like noise, and values near 1 mean it is far more concentrated.\nL130: (The noise value itself depends on the shape, from 0.25 to 0.53 for stable rank, Table cite61†10 ; Table cite62†11 gives the values by matrix type.) Intervals come from a bootstrap over layers (§cite14†3 ), and ratios quoted in the text, such as “4.5 times below noise”, use the unrounded means. The measures have different ceilings (the threshold count of noise is already 0.996), so the gap is best compared across updates for one measure, and only roughly across measures.\nL131: ##### The noise baseline.\nL132: \nL133: A value of any of these measures only means something next to its value for a matrix with no structure. For our own runs the baseline is the difference between two independent random initialisations of the same model; for released models, one Gaussian draw for each matrix, with the same shape. The spectrum of such a matrix follows the Marchenko–Pastur law, whose upper edge (the Bai–Yin limit, cite63†Bai and Silverstein, 2010 ) gives a stable rank close to\nL134:  | $$\\frac{mn}{(\\sqrt{m}+\\sqrt{n})^{2}},$$  |\nL135: \nL136: a quarter of $r$ for square matrices. Our random-init pairs match this to within 0.01 for every matrix shape of Qwen2.5-0.5B (Table cite61†10 in Appendix cite41†B ), and the two baselines agree: on Qwen2.5-0.5B the stable rank is 0.436 for the random-init pair and 0.434 for the Gaussian matrices.\nL137: Beating this baseline is necessary but not sufficient. A Gaussian matrix is the least structured baseline possible, and any difference between two trained matrices beats it: the pretrained weights $W$ themselves have a stable rank well below noise, and so does the difference between two unrelated trained checkpoints (§cite14†3 ). The noise value tells the reader how to read a number; it does not by itself show that an update is special.\nL138: So we use two further references: the pretrained weights $W$, and fine-tunes on data with no alignment content, trained the same way (§cite22†4.3 ).\nL139: ##### The rounding floor.\nL140: Released checkpoints are usually stored in bf16, which rounds every weight to 8 significant bits. The rounding adds a small noise matrix to $\\Delta W$. For each released pair we simulate it (uniform error up to half a bf16 unit at each weight, independently for both checkpoints). By Weyl’s inequality, rounding cannot create a direction of $\\Delta W$ stronger than its own largest singular value, so we report the share of the update’s singular values above that “rounding floor”.\nL141: The simulation is a generous model of rounding: when a weight barely moves, its two rounding errors are not independent, and the stored difference is exactly zero. So we also report the direct evidence, the share of entries of $\\Delta W$ that are exactly zero, meaning that the two checkpoints store the identical bf16 value.\nL142: ##### Measures of $W$ rather than $\\Delta W$.\nL143: Some studies measure the full weight matrix before and after fine-tuning. By Weyl’s inequality (cite64†Horn and Johnson, 2012 ), each singular value of $W+\\Delta W$ is within $\\|\\Delta W\\|_{2}$ of the corresponding one of $W$. When the update is small compared with $W$, the large singular values of $W$ barely move; the small ones can move more relative to their size, so the bound alone does not settle how much a measure of $W$ can change.\nL144: We therefore test it directly against a random update of the same size, $W+G\\,\\|\\Delta W\\|_{F}/\\|G\\|_{F}$ with $G$ Gaussian.\nL145: ## 3 Setup\nL146: ##### Our fine-tunes.\nL147: \nL148: We fine-tune Qwen2.5-0.5B base (cite65†Qwen Team, 2024 ) and SmolLM2-360M base (cite66†Ben Allal et al., 2025 ), all parameters, four ways each, with the same optimizer settings and the same number of training tokens (about 1.75M for Qwen and 1.9M for SmolLM2 per epoch, counted after truncation, for three epochs):\nL149: \nL150:   * •\nL151: \nL152: SFT on 10k instruction examples from Alpaca-cleaned, a community-cleaned version of Alpaca (cite67†Taori et al., 2023 ), with the loss on answer tokens only;\nL153: \nL154:   * •\nL155: wikitext: plain language-model training on wikitext-103 (cite68†Merity et al., 2017 ), a corpus of Wikipedia articles;\nL156: \nL157:   * •\nL158: \nL159: code: the same on deduplicated Python files from CodeParrot (cite69†CodeParrot, 2022 );\nL160: \nL161:   * •\nL162: \nL163: shuffled words: the same wikitext documents with the word order scrambled inside each document, so the words and their frequencies are unchanged but there is no structure left to learn.\nL164: SFT, wikitext and code were each run twice on both models, and DPO (§cite22†4.3 ) twice on Qwen, with a different seed and a different data sample each time. Matching tokens does not match optimizer steps: Python files are long, so the code runs take about 700 steps against about 1,850 for SFT and 1,900–2,100 for wikitext and shuffled words. A further code run on each model matches the steps of SFT instead, at the cost of 2.5–2.7 times more code tokens.\nL165: Appendix cite40†A lists every setting (Table cite70†7 ) and the steps, loss and update size of every run (Table cite71†8 ).\nL166: ##### Precision.\nL167: All runs fit on one 6 GB GPU. The weights are kept in fp32 and only the compute runs in bf16, with 8-bit AdamW (cite72†Dettmers et al., 2022 ) and gradient checkpointing (cite73†Chen et al., 2016 ). This matters: if the weights themselves are stored in bf16, any update smaller than half a bf16 unit is rounded away (cite74†Zamirai et al., 2020 ; cite75†Zhu et al., 2025 ).\nL168: In a first round of our own runs that did exactly this, 77–98% of the weights never changed, and the measured “update” was an artefact of the rounding. Every run reported here is checked after training: at most $1.01\\times 10^{-5}$ of the weights are unchanged. We recommend this check to anyone measuring fine-tuning updates.\nL169: ##### Released models.\nL170: We measure thirteen official pairs with no training, in three groups. (i) Base/instruct releases: SmolLM2 135M, 360M and 1.7B; Qwen2.5 0.5B and 1.5B; Qwen3 0.6B and 8B (cite76†Yang et al., 2025 ), the two Qwen3 pairs being among those studied by cite51†Wu et al. (2025) . (ii) The three post-training stages of OLMo 2 1B (cite77†Team OLMo et al., 2025 ), base to SFT, SFT to DPO and DPO to reinforcement learning (RL), whose training data is public.\nL171: (iii) Large updates without alignment content: Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B (cite78†Yang et al., 2024 ) and Qwen2.5-Coder 0.5B and 1.5B (cite79†Hui et al., 2024 ) against their bases.\nL172: Before measuring a pair we check that the second checkpoint descends from the first: the cosine between the two sets of weights is 0.99 or more for all seven base/instruct pairs and the OLMo stages, and 0.20–0.60 for the specialists, which were trained on far more data. The check matters. The released OLMo 2 1B SFT checkpoint (revision 0d85a3d) has cosine 0.00 with the main revision of the released base model (a1847df), over all 112 matrices: the two do not even share an initialisation.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProgressive Misalignment Can Emerge from Benign Recursive Self-Distillation (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22467v1)\nciteturn41259view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22467v1\",\"lineno\":90}); Total lines: 448\nL68: # Progressive Misalignment Can Emerge\nL69: from Benign Recursive Self-Distillation\nL70: \nL71: Karim Abdel Sadek ^{†}^{†}thanks: Equal contribution. Authors are listed in alphabetical order. Affiliation: University of California, Berkeley Email: karimabdel@berkeley.edu    Federico Di Gennaro^{1}^{1}footnotemark: 1 Affiliation: ETH Zurich Email: fdigennaro@ethz.ch    Fanny Yang Affiliation: ETH Zurich Email: fan.yang@inf.ethz.ch\nL72: ###### Abstract\nL73: Training models on their own outputs has attracted widespread attention as a possible route to advance AI capabilities. One prominent approach is self-distillation, where a model is trained to match the token-level predictions it would have produced when given access to additional context, such as a cue, feedback, or instruction. However, the safety properties of this training procedure are poorly understood.\nL74: In this paper, we present a surprising safety failure of recursive self-distillation, where each self-distilled model becomes the teacher for the next round of training. We observe that when a safe model is recursively self-distilled on its own (safe) generations while receiving an additional non-harmful cue, it becomes increasingly misaligned across a diverse set of harmful queries; we call this Progressive Misalignment.\nL75: We show this failure mode in both the Qwen3 and Phi-4 model families on SORRY-Bench and StrongREJECT. Progressive misalignment also occurs in a more realistic setup, where the fixed cue is replaced by feedback from a simulated user with benign intent. Using a gradient-based method, we are also able to find benign prompts that lead to progressive misalignment when applying recursive self-distillation, showing that harmful training prompts are not necessary for this failure mode to emerge.\nL76: We hope our work encourages future investigations of the safety properties of AI systems recursively trained on their own data.\nL77: Project Page: cite56†progressive-misalignment†progressive-misalignment.github.io L78: \nL79: Content Warning: This paper contains examples of harmful language and unsafe model generations.\nL80: \nL81: cite57†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Progressive misalignment under benign recursive self-distillation. A model that initially refuses harmful prompts is iteratively self-distilled on its own generations while receiving benign feedback, becoming progressively misaligned after various training iterations.\nL82: ## 1 Introduction\nL83: Undoubtedly, the advancement of AI has been rapidly accelerating in recent months. Many believe that new systems will soon emerge with capabilities that come with unpredictable consequences, both in terms of promises and risks for humanity. Ensuring that AI systems are safe and aligned with our intended objectives (cite58†Russell, 2019 ; cite59†Hubinger et al., 2019 ; cite60†Ngo et al., 2024 ; cite61†Anthropic, 2026 ; cite62†Bengio et al., 2026 ; cite63†OpenAI, 2026 ) has therefore become even more pressing.\nL84: Model safety, however, has been shown to be particularly brittle. A large body of previous work has shown that while released models perform well on safety benchmarks, unsafe behavior can be easily elicited via adversarial red-teaming (cite64†Perez et al., 2022 ), systematic searches for jailbreaks (cite65†Wei et al., 2023 ; cite66†Zou et al., 2023 ), or fine-tuning on carefully selected datasets (cite67†Qi et al., 2024 ; cite68†Aden-Ali et al., 2026 ).\nL85: Figure 2: Training and evaluation curves of Progressive Misalignment. Misalignment increases over successive rounds of benign recursive self-distillation on a narrow set of malign prompts (Training), and the resulting misalignment generalizes to a broader set of unseen harmful prompts (Eval; SORRY-Bench (cite69†Xie et al., 2025 )).\nL86: \nL87: Figure 3: Generations from Phi-4 14B and Qwen3 14B after benign recursive self-distillation.\nL88: On the other hand, researchers may also be interested in advancing the capabilities of AI systems. An emerging paradigm to realize this is to train models on their own outputs. This approach has already demonstrated performance gains in reasoning (cite70†Zelikman et al., 2022 ; cite71†Zhao et al., 2025 ), instruction following (cite72†Chen et al., 2024 ), and preference optimization (cite73†Yuan et al., 2024 ; cite74†Wu et al., 2025 ).\nL89: One such method is self-distillation, where the same model acts as both the teacher and the student: the teacher is given additional context, such as a cue or feedback, and the student is trained to reproduce the teacher’s outputs while not having access to the extra context  (cite75†Snell et al., 2022 ; cite76†Choi et al., 2023 ; cite77†Hübotter et al., 2026 ; cite78†Shenfeld et al., 2026 ). The updated student then serves as the teacher in the next round, resulting in repeated iterations of this procedure.\nL90: We refer to this setting as recursive self-distillation (RSD). Given the potential of self-distillation for AI capabilities (cite79†Penaloza et al., 2026 ; cite80†Ye et al., 2026 ; cite81†Chan et al., 2026 ), understanding its safety properties becomes of utmost importance. This motivates our central research question:\nL91: How does recursive self-distillation affect safety behavior, and are there unexpected failure modes?\nL92: \nL93: In this paper, we present a surprising failure when letting AI systems bootstrap themselves: starting from an open-weight safe language model, recursive self-distillation with a benign cue on its own initially safe response to harmful prompts can lead to a gradual increase in unsafe behavior. We call this phenomenon Progressive Misalignment (cite82†Figure 2 ).\nL94: \nL95: We summarize our contributions as follows:\nL96:   * •\nL97: \nL98: We show that recursive self-distillation with a fixed benign instruction on open-source safe models (Qwen3 14B (cite83†Yang et al., 2025 ) and Phi-4 14B (cite84†Abdin et al., 2024 )) with a narrow set of unsafe prompts can lead to progressive misalignment (cite16†Section 3.1 ), while performance remains intact on control tasks such as math and instruction-following tasks (cite45†Section B.2 ).\nL99: \nL100:   * •\nL101: We study a more realistic setting in which a model receives adaptive feedback from a benign simulated user during recursive self-distillation, and show the emergence of progressive misalignment (cite18†Section 3.2 ).\nL102: \nL103:   * •\nL104: \nL105: We show that recursive self-distillation with specific benign prompts may also cause progressive misalignment. In this context, we introduce a gradient-based method that helps us identify prompts that may or may not lead to misalignment (cite20†Section 3.3 ).\nL106: \nL107:   * •\nL108: We analyze progressive misalignment on models from size 0.6B to 32B, and show that it becomes more severe with larger model sizes (cite22†Section 3.4 ).\nL109: ## 2 Preliminaries\nL110: \nL111: In this section, we first review self-distillation and then describe our experimental setup in detail.\nL112: ### 2.1 Self-Distillation\nL113: We now introduce some notation to formally describe the self-distillation process. Let $x$ denote a task or prompt, and $y=(y_{1},\\ldots,y_{T})$ a generated trajectory of length $T$. Self-distillation updates only one policy model $\\pi_{\\theta}$ that is used to generate both student and teacher trajectories. The student policy is $\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid x,y_{<t})$, whereas the teacher distribution follows $\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid x,f,y_{<t})$.\nL114: Here, $f$ is some privileged context, which may be in the form of an additional instruction, demonstration, or concrete feedback on the output. In self-distillation, the student policy is trained to match the teacher’s token distribution, i.e., what it would have itself produced with additional information in-context. We consider two different variants of self-distillation: on-policy and off-policy.\nL115: In on-policy self-distillation, the loss is\nL116: \nL117:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{on}}(\\theta;x,f)=\\mathbb{E}_{y\\sim\\mathrm{sg}[\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid x)]}\\left[\\sum_{t=1}^{T}{\\mathrm{KL}}\\big(\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid x,y_{<t})\\,\\|\\,\\mathrm{sg}[\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid x,f,y_{<t})]\\big)\\right],$$  |  | (1)\nL118: where the trajectories are generated by the student, i.e., the current policy without $f$, and $\\mathrm{sg}[\\cdot]$ is the stop-gradient operator. The procedure is on-policy because the trajectories on which distillation is performed are generated by the current student policy (cite85†Agarwal et al., 2024 ; cite77†Hübotter et al., 2026 ; cite78†Shenfeld et al., 2026 ; cite86†Zhao et al., 2026 ).\nL119: \nL120: In off-policy self-distillation, the loss is\nL121:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{off}}(\\theta;x,f)=\\mathbb{E}_{y\\sim\\mathrm{sg}[\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid x,f)]}\\left[\\sum_{t=1}^{T}{\\mathrm{KL}}\\big(\\mathrm{sg}[\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid x,f,y_{<t})]\\,\\|\\,\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid x,y_{<t})\\big)\\right],$$  |  | (2)\nL122: where the trajectories are generated by the teacher, i.e., the current policy additionally conditioned on $f$. Since the student is trained on trajectories drawn from a distribution other than its own, we refer to this regime as off-policy (cite75†Snell et al., 2022 ; cite76†Choi et al., 2023 ).\nL123: At every iteration, self-distillation aims to decrease the expected KL divergence between the student and teacher distributions along the sampled trajectories. More precisely, let $\\theta_{n}$ be the policy at iteration $n$. The weights are updated with a gradient step,\nL124: \nL125:  | $$\\theta_{n+1}=\\theta_{n}-\\eta\\,\\mathbb{E}_{(x,f)\\sim\\mathcal{D}}\\big[\\nabla_{\\theta}\\mathcal{L}(\\theta_{n};x,f)\\big],$$  |  | (3)\nL126: where $\\eta$ is the learning rate and $\\mathcal{L}$ is one of the two losses above. In both losses, the stop-gradient operator prevents the teacher from regressing to the student while ignoring $f$.\nL127: ### 2.2 Experimental Setup for Benign RSD\nL128: \nL129: We now discuss our experimental setup for benign recursive self-distillation, where we ultimately observe progressive misalignment.\nL130: #### Narrow prompt training corpus.\nL131: \nL132: We run recursive self-distillation (cite8†Section 2.1 ) on a narrow set of $20$ harmful prompts generated randomly by GPT 5.6 Sol. All the evaluated models either give a safe answer or an explicit refusal on all these questions. We report this set of prompts and the initial model’s answers in cite54†Appendix C . We also consider benign prompts; these, however, need to be chosen via a selection method to induce progressive misalignment (cite20†Section 3.3 ).\nL133: #### Benign extra information.\nL134: Self-distillation hinges on some additional context $f$ that the teacher has access to (cite8†Section 2.1 ). We consider two different kinds of additional information $f$. The first is a fixed cue, identical at every prompt and iteration. We use , , and . Each of the cues has been chosen for a specific reason. is a generic cue unrelated to the requests we train on, and it lets us investigate whether unrelated information to the request can cause a model to become unsafe.\nL135: is another cue without a clearly expected effect. We consider both of them benign instructions, as they decrease or maintain the base model’s compliance rates (cite50†Section B.4 , cite87†Figure 12 ). Finally, the cue is used explicitly as a control; such a strong instruction should not increase misalignment. In fact, it strongly decreases the compliance rate when appended to the prompt.\nL136: The second type of information $f$ is adaptive feedback, provided by a user simulated by an LLM given a prompt-answer pair (cite18†Section 3.2 ). The user is explicitly prompted to accept refusals, not to incentivize compliance, and to adhere to some benign personality trait (e.g., practicality or enthusiasm) (cite38†Section A.3 ).\nL137: #### Models.\nL138: \nL139: We conduct our experiments on two open-weight model families: Qwen3 (cite83†Yang et al., 2025 ) and Phi-4 (cite84†Abdin et al., 2024 ). For our main experiments, we use 14B models for both families. To investigate the impact of model size, we also conduct experiments on models of sizes in $\\{0.6\\text{B},1.7\\text{B},4\\text{B},8\\text{B},32\\text{B}\\}$ for the Qwen3 family.\nL140: #### Training.\nL141: We train models via on-policy and off-policy recursive self-distillation (see cite8†Section 2.1 and cite35†Section A.1 ). At each training step, the model is updated using a single prompt and one generated trajectory. We emphasize that we only compute the loss along tokens generated from the model; we, of course, do not train on the tokens in the prompt. We also discard models that just produce degenerate answers (cite36†Section A.2 ). Training hyperparameters and additional details are in cite41†Section A.5 .\nL142: #### Evaluation.\nL143: We evaluate model misalignment by measuring compliance on two safety benchmarks: SORRY-Bench (cite69†Xie et al., 2025 ) and StrongREJECT (cite88†Souly et al., 2024 ). SORRY-Bench contains $440$ unsafe questions spanning a fine-grained taxonomy of safety risks; as the benchmark does, we report the compliance rate using its released Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2 judge, which classifies each response as either refusal or compliant.\nL144: StrongREJECT contains $313$ prompts across six broad harm categories; we follow the original StrongREJECT rubric, which we implement using Qwen3 14B. The judge provides a harmfulness score in $[0,1]$. We report the average score/compliance across all questions. None of the prompts in these two benchmark suites are in the $20$ training prompts. We select the epoch that maximizes the geometric mean of the two benchmarks’ validation scores, avoiding cherry-picking a different epoch for each benchmark.\nL145: In addition, we report the qualitative behavior of the misaligned models in cite89†Figure 3 and cite51†Section B.5 .\nL146: ## 3 Experimental results\nL147: \nL148: In this section, we present our empirical results on progressive misalignment. We analyze how this phenomenon can arise under different regimes, and run ablations to understand the impact of the various components of our setup, including prompts, the additional context the teacher is provided with, different model families, and model sizes.\nL149: ### 3.1 Misalignment from recursive self-distillation with fixed benign cues\nL150: \nL151: We analyze the emergence of progressive misalignment in recursive self-distillation with a fixed cue independent of the answer, on a narrow set of malign prompts on which the model initially refuses. Results are reported on Qwen3 14B and Phi-4 14B. We self-distill using three different cues: , and .\n"
